<header><p class="enc-kicker">Person</p><h1 class="enc-title">Adalbero of Reims</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates">d. <a href="/years/989">989</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/10th-century">10th century</a> · Also known as Adalbero, Archbishop of Reims</p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">Adalbero was Archbishop of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/reims">Reims</a> from 969 until his death in 989. After the death of the last Carolingian king, Louis V, in 987, he led the assembly of nobles and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/bishop">bishops</a> that elected <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/hugh-capet">Hugh Capet</a> as king of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/frankish-kingdom">Franks</a> instead of the Carolingian claimant <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/charles-duke-of-lower-lorraine">Charles of Lorraine</a>, and he crowned him. He was also a patron of learning, employing the scholar <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/pope-sylvester-ii">Gerbert of Aurillac</a>, the future Pope Sylvester II.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/adalbero-of-reims.jpg" alt="Adalbero of Reims" width="320" height="262" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: AnonymousUnknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Sacre_Hugues_Capet_1.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
